\DOC X86_BASIC_STEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_BASIC_STEP_TAC : thm option array -> string -> term ref option -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Turns an {eventually x86 P s} goal into an implication whose antecedent equates
an explicit state-update expression (the symbolic effect of the single next
instruction) with a fresh state variable, and whose conclusion is the original
{eventually} goal at that new state.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes {decode_ths : thm option array}, the array of pre-decoded
instruction theorems from which the next instruction's effect is obtained;
{sname : string}, the base name used for the fresh successor state variable in
the resulting implication; and {store_inst_term_to : term ref option}, an
optional reference cell in which the instruction term can be stored.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P s
   ========================================  X86_BASIC_STEP_TAC decode_ths sname store_inst_term_to
   A ?- u = s' ==> eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is a new state variable named from {sname}, and {u} is a
nested {write}-expression over the current state {s} describing the register,
flag, memory and event-trace updates of the one instruction at the current
program point, with reads of {s} left symbolic. The body {P} of the
{eventually} is carried over unaltered.

\COMMENTS
Every observed trace shows this single transformation, applied to goals whose
conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement about an x86 machine state.

\ENDDOC
